---
// The hub, shipped in 0.5.0: optional and self-hosted; the landing's one place for it (the wdx doc SITE, steps F and G).
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<section class="sec band tight"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="hub"><div class="hub-copy"><span class="tag">NEW IN 0.5.0</span><span class="eyebrow">Hub</span><h2>Your machines, one account.</h2><p class="lede">Log your machines in to a hub you run, and reach each one's agents, terminals and TIDE from any other with <code>--node</code>. The hub passes the connection on but cannot read it: TLS runs between the two machines, and the machine checks its own authenticator code.</p>
    <ul class="hub-points">
      <li>Remote access is off on a machine until you set up its code there.</li>
      <li>A project's docs can be pushed to the hub, encrypted at rest.</li>
      <li>Self-hosted, for you or your organisation: a Linux server with one command, or Fly.io.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <pre class="term"><span class="p">$</span> wdx hub login --url https://hub.example.com
<span class="d">logged in to https://hub.example.com as alice; this machine is node desk</span>

<span class="p">$</span> wdx hub node list
NAME         ONLINE  LAST SEEN            VERSION  OS            ID
desk (this)  yes     2026-11-08 15:33:10  0.5.0    linux/amd64   node-1a2b3c4d
laptop       yes     2026-11-08 15:32:58  0.5.0    darwin/arm64  node-5e6f7a8b

<span class="p">$</span> wdx tide --node laptop
<span class="d">on node laptop</span>
code for laptop: <span class="s">492133</span></pre>
  <div class="hub-foot"><p>The hub is optional: wdx works the same without one. There is no hosted hub to sign up to; you run <code>wdx-hub</code> yourself.</p>
    <div class="hub-links"><a class="more" href={`${base}/docs/guides/hub/`}>Use a hub →</a><a class="more" href={`${base}/docs/guides/running-a-hub/`}>Run a hub →</a></div></div></div>
</div></section>
